<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Feb 19, 2023">
<CPscript title="Polynomial-Like Rational Maps" level="min" >
<description>Polynomials may be distinguished among the rational maps by the fact that half their branching occurs at a single branch point. This gives us an opportunity to build discrete analogues. See "Polynomial.xmd" for a more direct construction.</description>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="button_ok.png">act 2;Cleanse;Map -x;infile_read example.p;set_Vlist 16 20 50 23;Disp -w -c -cfc5 Vlist; </cmd>
<text> Rational maps have the form R(z) = F(z)/G(z) where F and G are complex polynomials. Observe that if G is constant, then R is just a polynomial and half its branching occurs at infinity: more precisely, if R has degree n, then it has n-1 branch points in the finite plane and another n-1 at infinity. The converse is also true. I will let the reader work this out with the help of Appendix B.

Now, if we pre- and post-compose R with Mobius transformations of the sphere, then the result remains a rational function. It may no longer be a polynomial, but half the branching still occurs at a single point. We call rational functions with this property "polynomial-like".

We can build examples in the discrete setting for the following reason: if half the branching occurs at a single circle C, then C lies on all sheets of the image packing. That in turn means that we can map C to the outside of the unit disc to put all the rest of the circles inside, making the neighbors of C horocycles. The rest of the packing, in fact, is the image packing for a finite Blaschke product.

This script starts with an example, which we then deconstruct in exactly this way. Using methods developed in "Blaschke.cps", we can then construct an endless variety of discrete polynomial-like rational functions. </text>
<Section title="Deconstructing example1.p">
<text> The packing of p2 is displayed with its branch circles in blue. The first task is to find the number 'n' of the branch circle which is "on every sheet" --- in other words, there is no other circle or interstice that overlaps it.

Once you have found 'n', issue the command "V:=n" to store it in the CirclePack variable 'V'. The next command will then map it to the outside of the unit disc, and the command after that will remove it and project the rest of the circles to the hyperbolic plane. </text>
<cmd iconname="kivio.png">act 1;copy -p2 1;cir_invert -u _V;puncture _V;geom_to_h;layout;set_Vlist {c:s.gt.3.0};Disp -w -c -cfc5 Vlist; </cmd>
<text> You should take a little time to investigate this packing of the unit disc. The remaining branch circles should be blue, there are three of them. Two are simple branch points, one is order 2, meaning that the neighbors wrap three times around it. Which vertex is that? Knowing this about the interior branch points, what is the number of windings of the boundary horocycles around the inside of the unit circle? How does this relate to the order of branching of the circle you removed? (Picture it as the exterior of the disc on the sphere.)

Finally, copying p1 to p0 and max packiing there, we see an image familiar from "Blaschke.xmd". In particular, if you look for the branch points and click some boundary circles you will see that the map from p0 to p1 is a discrete finite Blaschke product. </text>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">act 0;copy -p1 0;max_pack;Disp -w -c -cfc5 Vlist;set_screen -p1 -d;Map 0 1; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Constructions">
<cmd iconname="network.png">act 1;cleanse -p0;cleanse -p1;infile_read rand70.p;Disp -w -c;copy 0;Disp -p0 -w -c;set_Vlist </cmd>
<text> The construction combines the methods used in "Blaschke.xmd" and the projection to the sphere in "MaxSphPack.xmd". The previous command has just loaded a random maximal packing. Mousing over an interior circle in p0 and pressing 'b' makes it into a branch point --- p1 is now a branched maximal packing. 

Moving p1 to the unit sphere and adding the outside of the unit disc as the new neighbor of all the boundary horocycles gives the image packing. If you have put in N-1 branch points, then the boundary horocycles wrap N times around the inside of the unit circle; this means the outer circle we added has branch order N-1. In other words, as we expect, we get half the branching by one circle. </text>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">geom_to_s -p1;add_ideal -p1;Disp -p1 -w -c -cfc5 Vlist  -cfc195 M </cmd>
<text> Put the associated maximal packing in p0, and we have a discrete rational mapping from p0 to p1. 'NSpole' may be applied to normalize the packings by pressing 's' --- this just applies Mobius transformations to make the distribution of circles more visually balanced in each packing. The added caps are red.

You might try this yourself with a random or other packing to get a feel for the construction. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">cleanse -p0;copy -p1 0;max_pack -p0;Disp -p0 -w -c -cfc5 Vlist -cfc195 M; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="b" mnemonic="b" tooltip="choose a branch point" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">set_Vlist z #XY;set_aim -p1 4.0 Vlist[0];set_rad -p1 5.0 b;repack -p1 2000;layout -p1;set_Vlist -p1 {c:a.gt.3};Disp -p1 -w -c -cfc5 Vlist;Disp -p0 -w -c -cfc5 Vlist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="s" mnemonic="s" tooltip="normalize on the sphere" iconname="editdelete.png">NSpole -p1;disp -p1 -wr;NSpole -p0;disp -p0 -wr </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
